# HexLinux — keyboards readable by the user of the active local session.
#
# Installed by install-udev-rules.sh as /etc/udev/rules.d/70-hexlinux.rules.
#
# Why: holding the dictation shortcut is seen by reading the keyboards under
# /dev/input, which only root and the "input" group can do by default. The
# "uaccess" tag asks systemd-logind to grant the user who sits at the machine
# an ACL on each keyboard, and to take it back when that user logs out or
# another one takes the seat. The number sits below 73 on purpose:
# 73-seat-late.rules is where the tag is turned into the ACL.
#
# What it grants, said plainly: read AND write access to every keyboard, for
# every program of that user, not only HexLinux. Any of them can then read what
# is typed — passwords included — and inject keystrokes by writing to the
# device. That is the price of hold-to-talk on Linux. HexLinux itself only
# reads, never grabs a keyboard, and looks at nothing but its shortcut's keys.
#
# Without this rule, dictation still works through `hexlinux --toggle` bound to
# a desktop shortcut. Never add MODE or GROUP here: either would outlive the
# session and reach every user.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ENV{ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD}=="1", TAG+="uaccess"
